These match the corresponding final66-artifact build-release files.

25 actual QEMU/OVMF firmware scenarios passed, with disposable USB disk files.
11 scenarios ran on each of x86-64 and IA32:
1. Full18,874,368-byte disk payload: write, flush and complete readback.
2. User declines ERASE confirmation: complete target unchanged.
3. Non-removable target excluded: complete target unchanged.
4. Read-only target excluded: complete target unchanged.
5. Corrupt source SHA256 rejected: complete target unchanged.
6. Target USB removed after confirmation prompt: fresh protocol lookup fails,
   complete target unchanged; no stale protocol pointer is dereferenced.
7. Target exactly one sector larger than512-byte payload: tail cleanup does
   not overlap the payload and the single extra sector is zero.
8. Target exactly equal to512-byte payload: no out-of-range tail write.
9. Utility booted from removable USB: boot/source device excluded; a second
   USB disk is detected even when firmware initially connected only boot USB.
10. Source system.zmedia on a different removable USB volume: source excluded,
    independent target written successfully.
11. Multiple source volumes: numbered source selection works and selected
    source disk is excluded from targets.

Additional IA32 scenario23: sparse USB target2TiB+1MiB,512-byte payload.
Actual firmware recognized2,199,024,304,128-byte capacity. The full64-bit block
address was passed using both ABI words. The final33 sectors were zeroed and
read back at their true high address; a seeded low-LBA alias region remained
unchanged. This catches truncation of the high LBA word without allocating a
multi-terabyte physical test medium.

Final integrated scenarios24 and25: both UEFI architectures.
Booted the exact final Zero USB payload from its own removable USB disk;
selected2 Flash Writer in the Zero menu; discovered a separate source volume;
loaded final system.zmedia (SHA256 below); selected and confirmed a third,
blank64MiB USB disk; wrote and verified its entire payload and cleared tail;
pressed Enter to return to the Zero menu; selected1 and reached the Zero OS
terminal. Both the original boot USB and source-file SHA256 stayed unchanged.

